- sys/types
' Shrine: the namespace
'
' We setup

'  Suppose a record lives at /a and another at /a/b/c, with no record
'  at /a/b. An x read of /a sees /a alone. A y read also sees /a/b/c: b
'  is transparent. If b acquires a record, y stops there; z still
'  reaches c. Care describes what a reader can observe at one path.
'
'  A make note supplies a myth. The store commits it as a saga with
'  version information. A later cull leaves a tombstone: at the new
'  case an x read answers null, while the earlier case still names the
'  old record. A path with no assertion can answer unknown. See
'  namespace/page for how a case selects a historical answer.

+  twig
  '  One slot-value pair: the unit a myth is built from.
  : slot=path =pail
+  myth
  '  The record at a node: slots (paths, used as opaque keys) to
  '  pails.  The unit of state the namespace versions and a behavior
  '  manipulates.  A myth holds a handful of slots, so it is an
  '  ordered map (/mop), not a tree — and a type alias, so the whole
  '  mop method set (get/put/size/uni) applies directly.
  mop[path pail lt_pith]
  +  new
    '  The empty myth.
    ^ myth
    (mop/empty)
  +  sing
    '  The one-slot myth.
    \ slot=path v=pail
    ^ myth
    | mop/sing[path pail lt_pith] slot v
  +  meld
    '  Union; the add side wins collisions (the default talk law).
    \ add=myth cur=myth
    ^ myth
    cur.uni(add)
  ' TODO: needs wet namespace-modules
  '  +  gett
  '    (@ v)
  '    \ slot=path mit=myth
  '    ^ maybe[v]
  '    mit.get(slot).map(v/try_from_pail)
  +  add
    '  Chain one twig onto a myth: mit.add(twig).
    \ =twig mit=myth
    ^ myth
    mit.put(twig.slot twig.pail)
+  lock
  '  One care's two independent local counters.  Data advances for
  '  every changed version of the care; shape advances only when the
  '  care's live domain changes.  Shape implies data, but is not a
  '  position in the data timeline.
  : data=nat shape=nat
+  yuga
  '  A node's versioning information: one lock per care, plus the
  '  apollo time and the PKI blockheight at mint.
  : x=lock
    y=lock
    z=lock
    now=nat
    block=nat
+  oath
  '  Unsigned security scaffolding. Both fields are zero in this port.
  '  Neither authentication nor predecessor-hash semantics are
  '  implemented; this layout is not a network verification contract.
  : sig=nat prev_hash=nat
+  aeon
  '  Versioning plus security: everything about a committed version
  '  except the record itself.
  : =yuga =oath
+  saga
  '  The committed version of a node: the aeon and the record.
  '  /sov/step adjusts the yuga as it applies diffs, so historical
  '  case queries are floor lookups, not subtree scans.
  '  The record rides behind a pin: each committed myth is one
  '  content-addressed unit, so snapshotting the sovereign
  '  re-serializes spines, never stored records (global.plan's
  '  small-pins discipline).  Version-only re-mints (ass/next over
  '  the held record) share the old pin verbatim.
  : =aeon myth=pin[myth]
+  note
  '  One diff: replace the record, merge into it, or retract the
  '  node.  Versioning is the namespace's job — a diff carries none.
  +  make
    : =myth
  +  poke
    : =myth
  +  cull
    :
  & note/make
    note/poke
    note/cull
+  care
  '  Read restriction: the node only (.x), the node plus its frontier
  '  — the nearest record-bearing descendants, with transparent paths
  '  passing through (.y) — or the whole subtree (.z).
  & care/x
    care/y
    care/z

  +  x
    :

  +  y
    :

  +  z
    :

  +  seg
    '  The care's /h path segment.
    '  ?=  ((care/seg .z) ($ts "z"))
    \ c=care
    ^ iota
    ? c
     > .x  'x
     > .y  'y
     > .z  'z
+  dep
  '  One cared path: a (care, path) pair.  News carries them as
  '  subscriptions (p is the subscriber); hear arms receive them as
  '  changes (p is the changed path).
  : c=care p=path
+  hunt
  '  A cared path: the (care, path) pair that names both a place and
  '  a reach — .x the node alone, .y the node plus its direct kids,
  '  .z the whole subtree.  A face's rendering claim is one, riding
  '  as DATA at ['hunt] on the face record itself (a pail slot, so
  '  it is explorer-visible and pokeable live): p is spelled
  '  relative to the face's own mount root ([] = the root itself),
  '  so the claim survives mounting anywhere and needs no rebase.
  : c=care p=path
+  safe
  '  A version-pinned cared read: the target's PER-CARE local DATA
  '  case (not a sovereign epoch), the care, and the path.  A safe
  '  names an immutable answer — its /h spelling (safe/spell) never
  '  changes meaning once minted — so a record slot holding one is
  '  a pinned reference: a citation when left alone, a LIVE LINK
  '  when the record carries the fresh kook, which keeps every
  '  top-level safe slot current as its target changes.
  : ver=nat =care =path

  +  spell
    '  The /h path a safe dereferences through:
    '  /h/<care>/<ver>/<len>/<path>.
    '  ?= ((safe 3 .x ['a 'b]).spell ((\ p=path p) ['h 'x '3 '2 'a 'b]))
    \ s=safe
    ^ path
    = head=path  ['h (care/seg s.care) ($u s.ver) ($u s.path.len)]
    head.weld(s.path)

  +  of_hunt
    '  A safe over a hunt's (care, path) at a known case.
    \ ver=nat h=hunt
    ^ safe
    | safe ver h.c h.p
' Structural typing *of* the namespace.  Node-level enforcement is
' LIVE: a record carrying its lash at ['sys 'lash] is judged by the
' commit gate (guard_diffs and fit_check in namespace.foil). A write
' whose final record breaks its own contract rejects the whole event,
' except a write that CHANGES the lash (adoption over legacy data
' is allowed; the watch app's standing queries find the survivors).
' Soma/yoke subtree patterns and the dep side (fief) remain
' vocabulary only for now.
'
+  res
  '  A resolution result.  .null is a tombstone answer; .unknown is
  '  the absence of any answer.  Never collapse the two: a shrug is
  '  not a tombstone.
  (@@ v)
  & res/known[v]
    res/null
    res/unknown

  +  known
    : value=v

  +  null
    :

  +  unknown
    :

  +  same
    '  Extensional equality of resolution states.  Unknown and
    '  tombstone remain distinct; known values compare deeply, a
    '  pinned record by its content.
    \ a=res[v] b=res[v]
    ^ bool
    | equal a b

  +  run
    '  Map a function over a known value; null and unknown ride.
    (@ w)
    \ fun={\ v}w r=res[v]
    ^ res[w]
    ? r
     > (./known x)  (./known (fun x))
     > .null        .null
     > .unknown     .unknown

+  sign
  '  Per-care change grade for one path: 0 unchanged, 1 data moved,
  '  2 data and shape moved (a creation or removal; shape implies
  '  data within one commit).
  : x=nat y=nat z=nat


' A nominal reference is an exact x data moment, not a floating path.
+  flaw
  '  A type can keep its name while its definition changes. path and
  '  case identify the exact definition a stored value refers to. For a
  '  read of ordinary data at a chosen care, use safe instead.
  : path=path case=nat

  +  brand
    \ reference=flaw
    ^ nat
    | brand_encode reference.path reference.case
  +  parse
    \ brand=nat
    ^ maybe[flaw]
    > (./some decoded) (brand_decode brand) .none
    = [target version] decoded
    | ./some (flaw target version)
  +  matches
    \ held=pail reference=flaw
    ^ bool
    | eq (nominal_brand held) reference.brand


'  Shrine: event handling
'  Shrine has three active limbs: feet, hands, and wings.
'   A hand processes events, reacting to changes in itself, children,
'   and dependencies.
'   A foot is the imperative side of the IO subsystem
'   A wing is an index

+  kook
  '  The value of the well-known behavior slot: a list of form-node
  '  refs — namespace paths, each read directly at resolution.  Any
  '  node holding a form at ['sys 'form] is thereby a registry entry, so
  '  behaviors are rerootable; the node's registry lives beneath its
  '  publisher authority. Each reference is fully qualified.
  : refs=row[path]
+  wing
  '  The other value of the behavior slot: a record at
  '  /<owner>/idx/<name> holding a wing there and a hunt at the tack
  '  slot declares a backward index over that hunt.

  '  This is activated BeOS-style at commit. Only parents could read
  '  wings in progress, and the kernel never puts anything in the
  '  ancestors. The local root is the kernel, which sees dispatch.
  : file={\ path myth}myth
+  crew
  '  A node's dependencies: a map from SLOT to (care, path) dep.
  '  The key is purely indexical — a local name, exactly like a
  '  myth's slot keys — so behaviors find their views by name and
  '  the watched paths can move without them noticing.
  mop[path dep lt_pith]
  +  sing
    '  A crew of one: a single named dependency.
    \ slot=path d=dep
    ^ crew
    | mop/put[path dep lt_pith] slot d (mop/empty)
  +  pail
    '  The value of the well-known /sys/crew slot.
    : =crew
+  mode
  '  What kind of change happened at a path.
  & mode/add
    mode/dif
    mode/del

  +  add
    :

  +  dif
    :

  +  del
    :
+  writ
  '  An incoming command: replace the myth, merge into it, delete the
  '  node — or WARP it: apply a declared operation to the current
  '  record, the input riding the event and never the namespace.
  & writ/make
    writ/poke
    writ/cull
    writ/warp

  +  warp
    '  Poke-with-an-overlay: op names the op card (its ['sys 'op]
    '  slot holds the op record, fn inside), input is the transient
    '  argument record.  Dispatch applies (fn input cur) and commits
    '  the result as a REPLACE — the fn computed the whole new
    '  record, so slots it dropped vanish (a meld would resurrect
    '  them).  The commit gate judges the final record as for any
    '  write.
    : op=path input=myth

  +  make
    : =myth

  +  poke
    : =myth

  +  cull
    :
+  bowl
  '  Per-activation context.  now is a logical clock, +1 per
  '  activation within the event; kids is the in-flight care-y view
  '  of the node's own subtree; crew holds the hydrated dependency
  '  committed views declared in its /sys/crew slot, keyed by their
  '  indexical slot names.
  '  All hydrated reads retain assertions, including computed reads.
  : src=path
    here=path
    now=nat
    eny=nat
    kids=axal[myth]
    crew=mop[path axal[saga] lt_pith]
+  yell
  '  What an arm returns: cards (path-tagged writs) to emit and the
  '  node's new state.
  : cards=row[[p=path q=writ]] out=myth
+  form
  '  On creation start at init. A poke invokes talk; changes below a
  '  node reach take, and changes to its named dependencies reach hear.
  '  Each active arm returns a yell for the dispatcher.
  '  A behavior: six laws over (bowl, state, input).  dead and goof
  '  are vocabulary only in this port — never invoked.
  : init={\ bowl myth}yell
    talk={\ bowl myth myth}yell
    take={\ bowl myth axal[mode]}yell
    hear={\ bowl myth row[dep]}yell
    dead={\ bowl myth}yell
    goof={\ bowl myth}yell

'  Read a lash in two parts. A curb says which definition moments a
'  value in a slot may have. The need groups say which slots must
'  exist. For example, requiring either lede or about still allows a
'  record with only about; typing lede alone does not require it.
'
'  The contract travels on the record. For the write checks follow
'  fit_check and guard_diffs in namespace.foil: the candidate record
'  matters, so an operation that drops a required slot can fail even
'  when its input was well typed.

+  curb
  ' Exact definition moments admitted by one slot, as a union.
  row[flaw]
  +  of_brand
    \ brand=nat
    ^ curb
    > (./some reference) (flaw/parse brand) []
    [reference]
  +  of_type
    \ reflected=type
    ^ curb
    ? reflected
     > (./ref brand arguments) (curb/of_brand brand)
     > (./rec brand items)
      | curb/of_brand brand
     > (./sum variants)
      | row/fold[type curb] []
        (\ allowed=curb variant=type
          | row/weld (curb/of_type variant) allowed)
        variants
    []
+  curbs
  '  Slot -> curb typing for one record.  Types only what is
  '  present; presence lives in lash/need.  Open: slots absent from
  '  the map are unconstrained (records stay row-polymorphic).
  '  For example, typing status rules out a value of the wrong type
  '  when status is present. To require it, add [status] to lash/need.
  mop[path curb lt_pith]
+  lash
  '  R/W typing for the record at one name.  need is CNF presence:
  '  each inner row is a disjunction of slots (at least one present);
  '  the outer row conjoins the groups.  [[status]] = status required;
  '  [[lede about]] = lede-or-about.  lede and help document the
  '  name this lash specs — a one-line description and longer prose,
  '  text cords, 0 = absent (not /lore: that lives downstream in
  '  pact).
  : read=curbs
    write=curbs
    need=row[row[path]]
    lede=nat
    help=nat
  +  new
    '  The empty lash: no typing, no presence demands, no docs.
    ^ lash
    | lash (mop/empty) (mop/empty) [] 0 0
+  op
  '  One declared operation on a spec'd record: poke-with-an-overlay.
  '  fn is the operation itself — (fn input cur) computes the WHOLE
  '  next record, the input riding the event and never the
  '  namespace (lain's writ/warp applies it and commits the result
  '  as a replace; the gate judges the final record as usual).  The
  '  receiver and output name roles; the inputs document the input
  '  record's slots, types as documentation spellings.
  : name=iota
    receiver=iota
    inputs=row[[slot=path ty=nat]]
    output=iota
    lede=nat
    fn={\ myth myth}myth

'  These constructors can live in published records. Their brands must
'  remain readable when a new kernel opens an old publication. A
'  read-time view plan belongs in types.foil; the code that evaluates
'  it belongs in namespace.foil.
'
'  Read the get signatures by the shape they consume and return. knit
'  turns one record into a tree; felt folds a tree into one record.
'  dyed changes a record, sewn changes a tree. spun also sees the
'  view's own record, so two views of the same input can use different
'  arguments. patch is optional: check it before asking a stage to
'  patch.
'
'  A cell returns two answers. /x reads its data tree and /c/x reads
'  its context. Both are computed on read. See tests/cells.foil for
'  secondary inputs and the difference between these answers.

+  knit
  ' build structure
  : get={\ myth}axal[myth] patch=maybe[{\ myth}axal[myth]]
+  felt
  ' delete structure. inverse of +knit
  : get={\ axal[myth]}myth patch=maybe[{\ axal[myth]}myth]
+  dyed
  ' transform content
  : get={\ myth}myth patch=maybe[{\ myth}myth]
+  sewn
  ' transform structure
  : get={\ axal[myth]}axal[myth] patch=maybe[{\ axal[myth]}axal[myth]]
+  spun
  ' transform structure with the view node's own record as the args
  ' channel: the /x lens ctor.  get receives the node's record (its
  ' slots ARE the view's constant args) and the tack target's tree
  ' pinned at y care, and returns the derived tree. /sys/weft holds
  ' this ctor inline or an x-care safe to a ctor record. /sys/tack
  ' holds a y-care safe. The defining datum owns the result case.
  : get={\ myth axal[myth]}axal[myth]
    patch=maybe[{\ myth axal[myth]}axal[myth]]
+  cell
  ' The cell ctor: get receives the context (the cell's own slots,
  ' secondary inputs as views at ['views <slot>]) and the input tree
  ' the tack names, and returns context' and the data tree.  /x reads
  ' the tree, /c/x the context'; neither is retained.
  : get={\ myth axal[myth]}[myth axal[myth]]
+  cell_view
  ' A secondary input handed to a spun ctor: the tree a reference slot
  ' of the cell named, pinned, placed at ['views <slot>] in the record
  ' the ctor receives.
  : tree=pin[axal[myth]]
